# Minimal dashboard UI — plain HTML/CSS/JS

This is a data tool, not a marketing site. The job of the UI is to make
the categorization data (company, website, primary tag, secondary tags,
keywords) scannable at a glance and make submitting a new brand fast. Every
choice below serves that, not decoration for its own sake.

## Ground rules for "minimalistic but nice"
- Minimal means restraint in what's on screen, not lack of care in how
  it's done. Precision in spacing/type/alignment IS the design here —
  there's no signature illustration or hero moment to hide behind.
- One accent color, used sparingly (primary-tag chips, the submit button,
  focus states) — everything else is neutral. Do not reach for gradients,
  drop shadows on every card, or more than one accent hue.
- No default browser form styling and no unstyled `<table>` — but also no
  heavy component-library look (Bootstrap card shadows, rounded pill
  buttons everywhere). Build it plain, in your own CSS.

## Token system (pick once, use everywhere — don't restyle per-page)
Define these as CSS custom properties in `:root` before writing any
component so every page shares them:
- **Color**: a near-white or near-black background (pick one, not both —
  don't make the form page light and the Data page dark), one neutral
  text color at ~85% contrast (not pure #000/#FFF, it's harsh), one muted
  secondary-text color for metadata (website URLs, keyword chips), and
  ONE accent color used only for primary-tag chips + primary actions.
- **Type**: one system font stack is fine here (this is a utility tool,
  not a brand moment) — but set a real type scale (e.g. 14px body, 13px
  metadata, 20px page heading) rather than browser defaults everywhere.
  Monospace for the website/URL field specifically reads as "data tool,"
  and is a nice small intentional touch.
- **Spacing**: pick one base unit (8px is a safe default) and derive every
  margin/padding/gap as a multiple of it. Inconsistent spacing is the
  single fastest way a plain HTML/CSS page reads as unstyled.
- **Radius**: pick one border-radius value (0, or a small ~4-6px) and use
  it everywhere — chips, inputs, buttons, cards. Mixing sharp and rounded
  corners across the same page is a common tell of un-intentional design.

## Layout for THIS content specifically
- **Tag hierarchy in the UI should mirror the tag hierarchy in the data**:
  primary tag gets the accent color and sits first/largest; secondary tags
  are smaller, neutral-colored chips beside or below it. Don't render
  primary and secondary as visually identical chips — that throws away
  the one/many distinction your whole backend was built around.
  Keywords are the noisiest field (up to ~12 items) — render them as
  small, low-contrast chips or a single muted comma line, clearly
  visually subordinate to the tags. They're supporting evidence, not the
  headline.
- **Company rows/cards**: name + website first (website in the muted
  color, monospace), tags directly below in the hierarchy above, keywords
  last and visually quietest. Don't give every field equal visual weight —
  that's what makes a data table feel like a wall of text.
- **Empty and loading states are part of the design, not an afterthought**:
  a categorization call takes a few seconds (scrape + 2 LLM calls) — show
  a real loading state on submit, not just a frozen button. An empty Data
  page (no companies yet) should say plainly what to do next, not just
  show blank space.
- **Filter/search input** (you already have "filter by name or website")
  should sit clearly above the list it filters, full-width or near it —
  not a small corner input easy to miss.

## What to avoid specifically for this project
- Don't add animation beyond a simple fade-in or a subtle hover state on
  rows — a categorization tool doesn't need scroll effects or motion.
- Don't use more than one shade of "success/info" color for tag chips —
  resist the urge to color-code every distinct primary tag category
  differently; that turns into visual noise once you have 30+ categories,
  and undermines the "one accent color" rule above.
- Don't center-align body text or table cells — left-align everything
  except numeric/short badge content.

## Before calling it done
Check the page at actual mobile width (not just resize the desktop
browser slightly) — chips wrapping badly and cramped tables are the most
common breakage on data-heavy pages. Also check visible keyboard focus on
the form inputs and submit button — easy to lose by accident with custom
CSS resets.